-- Violations of the identity law, as the law actually states it.
--
-- CLAUDE.md, the first invariant: "Identical content = identical id, at every
-- tier, from every source." And: "Tiers are a FLOOR, not identity: same content =
-- same id at every tier; tier is never mixed into the hash ('Fine' as a one-word
-- reply IS the sentence IS the word — one id)."
--
-- This function checked ONE thing: that tier fell in 0..255. That is a column
-- range check, not the identity law. It returned zero rows on a substrate holding
-- 5,063 entities stored at TWO tiers each — 10,126 rows for 5,063 entities — and
-- so reported clean while the law was broken. A detector that cannot fail is
-- worse than no detector: it is a green light over the exact thing it is named
-- for.
--
-- The checks now:
--
--   multi_tier_entity      one content id with rows at more than one tier. The id
--                          is correct (content-addressed, tier never in the hash);
--                          the DUPLICATION is the violation. Observed as 2+3
--                          pairs: SemLink staged concept identifiers at word tier
--                          while PropBank/VerbNet/FrameNet staged the same
--                          identifiers at sentence tier, so one entity ended up
--                          with two rows carrying two different decompositions
--                          (mince.01 as m|i|n|c|e|.|0|1 and as mince|.|01).
--
--   ambiguous_physicality  one (entity_id, type, id) carrying MORE THAN ONE
--                          distinct trajectory. A physicality id must be a
--                          function of what it holds; when it is not, any
--                          DISTINCT ON over that key is settled by the query
--                          PLAN. Not hypothetical: it made constituents_closure
--                          return an 8-point decomposition for an id alone and a
--                          3-point one for the same id inside a 500-id batch, so
--                          render_text_batch produced different text depending on
--                          what else was in the array, and every realize_batch
--                          consumer inherited it.
--
--   tier_out_of_range      the original range check, kept.
--
-- Reports ids, not counts, so a caller can act on them. Deliberately unbounded: a
-- violation list that silently truncates is the same failure as a detector that
-- cannot fail.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION identity_law_violations()
RETURNS TABLE(id bytea, tier smallint, reason text)
LANGUAGE sql
STABLE
SET search_path = @extschema@, public
AS $$
    SELECT e.id, e.tier, 'tier_out_of_range'::text
    FROM @extschema@.entities e
    WHERE e.tier < 0 OR e.tier >= 256

    UNION ALL

    -- Same content id stored at more than one tier: one entity, N rows.
    SELECT e.id, e.tier, 'multi_tier_entity'::text
    FROM @extschema@.entities e
    JOIN (SELECT d.id FROM @extschema@.entities d
          GROUP BY d.id HAVING count(*) > 1) dup ON dup.id = e.id

    UNION ALL

    -- One physicality key holding more than one distinct trajectory, so the key
    -- does not determine its content and reads over it are plan-dependent.
    SELECT p.entity_id, NULL::smallint, 'ambiguous_physicality'::text
    FROM (SELECT ph.entity_id, ph.type, ph.id
          FROM @extschema@.physicalities ph
          WHERE ph.trajectory IS NOT NULL
          GROUP BY ph.entity_id, ph.type, ph.id
          HAVING count(DISTINCT public.ST_AsBinary(ph.trajectory)) > 1) p
$$;
